course: dictionary
lesson: 4248
title: Graphemes: letters that make sounds
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A grapheme is how a sound is written down. «A phoneme is one sound in a word; a grapheme is the letter or letters that write that sound.» You hear a phoneme, but you see a grapheme.
explain: A grapheme can be one letter, like s in sun, or a group of letters that make one sound. Look at «sh» in ship: two letters, but only one sound, so it is one grapheme. Look at «igh» in night: three letters, one sound, one grapheme.
explain: One sound can have more than one grapheme. The sound «ee» can be written ee in see, ea in sea, e in me or y in happy. When you read or spell, ask: which letters make this sound?
fact: A grapheme is a letter or group of letters that stands for one sound.
fact: «sh» is a digraph: two letters that make one sound, as in ship.
fact: «igh» is a trigraph: three letters that make one sound, as in night.
fact: A split grapheme has another letter between its two parts, as in a_e in cake.
fact: Grapheme–phoneme correspondence (GPC) means matching the letters on the page to the sounds you hear.
exercise: What is a grapheme? | A word that rhymes with another word | A word that means the same as another word | *A letter or group of letters that stands for one sound | A single sound you hear in a word
exercise: How many letters are in the grapheme «igh» in night? | Two | *Three | One | Four
exercise: Which word ends with the sound «ee» written as y? | me | sea | see | *happy
test: In the word «ship», which letters make one grapheme? | *sh | hi | s and h | ship
test: What is a phoneme? | *A single sound in a word | A single letter on the page | A whole word with its meaning | A group of two words
exam: Which of these is a split grapheme? | ea in sea | igh in night | sh in ship | *a_e in cake
